{
    "background_story": "Israel was a brilliant and eccentric artist, known for his vivid paintings that seemed to pulse with life. However, his creative genius was often hindered by his severe nearsightedness. Without his thick, black-rimmed glasses, the world was a blurry mess. Despite this, Israel learned to adapt, developing a unique perspective on the world. He would often remove his glasses to study the way colors blended together, using his impaired vision to create dreamy, impressionistic works. His art studio was a testament to his ingenuity, with magnifying glasses and special lenses scattered about. Israel's nearsightedness became an integral part of his artistic process, allowing him to see the world in a way that others did not. His paintings were a reflection of his inner world, full of soft focus and vibrant hues.",
